+# The contract the logger emits against is unsatisfiable
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+Blocks: 05. The pass it exists to run measures this contract, not the reading, so running it again before this lands buys another number about the same four things.
+
+The first log pass on the graph held **24 of 90** on `gpt-oss:20b`. Rescored on the document the engine would write, the 50 valid determined runs go from **7 to 30** held. Four things below, every one of them a prompt, a schema or an edge — none of them the model reading Italian.
+
+## What is wrong
+
+1. **A block cannot be emitted in part, and the brief forbids the one label that makes the rest of it honest.** `tools.strict_schema` marks every property required, so `log.PerformedBlock` arrives needing `rounds`, `rounds_source`, `rest`, `rest_source` and each item's `amount`, `amount_source`. Reporting *"8 ripetizioni invece di 12"* therefore means also writing a round count and a rest the user never gave. The honest label for those is `assumed` at the prescribed value — what `log.validate` accepts, and what every expected document in the set carries. `coach/logger.LOGGING` says *"Never `assumed`: that is the engine's"* nine lines before it says *"A number marked `assumed` has to be the prescribed one"*. A model that obeys the first writes `reported` on a number nobody said, which is the one mistake the same brief calls the one nothing downstream can catch.
+2. **A refusal ends the run.** `after_validate` sends a refused document to `END`. The planner's sends it back to the model with `FEEDBACK`, named violations and all, for up to `ATTEMPTS`. Every refusal here is about the document — an id that does not resolve, an `assumed` that contradicts the prescription, a `retest_date` that is not after today — and the logger has the tools and the messages to fix one on a second turn. In the suite a refused run loses the row twice: `VALIDATOR` and `DOCUMENT`.
+3. **The exclusion vocabulary is nowhere in the context.** `ProposedExclusion.value` has to be a `movement_pattern` or a `loaded_joints` value, character for character, or `exclusions.classify` refuses it. None of the logger's six tools carries either: `logger.KEEPS` is id, name, equipment, mechanic, primary muscles; `search_exercises` returns catalog records, which hold no pattern; `compare_variants` has both, for two ids the model must already have picked. Six rows turn on that one string (U13, U14, U15, U16, and A5 and A12 the other way).
+4. **The suite scores the form and not the fact.** `_difference` compares the emitted document field by field and starts at `len(blocks)`. A model that writes out the blocks the report did not mention — which clause 1 makes the path of least resistance — fails that check and every field after it, though `log.to_performed` resolves both documents to the same workout and `_produced` already costs the resolved one. `DOCUMENT` red under a green `VOLUME` is the signature, and the rescore is the size: 7 held on the emitted form, 30 on the resolved one.
+
+## What to build
+
+- **One rule about provenance in `LOGGING`, where there are two.** A value copied unchanged from the prescription is `assumed`; a value the user gave is `reported`; a value that came back from `ask_user` is `asked`. Say that the form requires the whole block, so copying is expected and labelling it is the point. The *"never `assumed`"* paragraph goes.
+- **A bounded refusal loop**, mirroring the planner: `after_validate` back to `logger` while attempts remain, with the refusal as a message naming it. **Two attempts, not three** — a refusal here is a typo or a provenance, and a second read either fixes it or nothing will, and each attempt is minutes of wall time on the local model. The run closes `REFUSED` only when the attempts are spent, and `records.closing` is still called exactly once.
+  - The run record already has the column: `record.of` reads `refusals` and writes them as `attempts`, in `plans.Violation`'s `rule`/`says` shape. Give `log`'s refusal that pair rather than teach the record a second shape.
+- **The exclusion vocabulary in the brief, read from the catalog**: `catalog.values(connection, "movement_pattern")` and `catalog.joint_values(connection)` — never a constant, the standing rule, and the same two reads `classify` already does. In the brief rather than a seventh tool: it is twenty words, and on the local model a tool call is a minute somebody waits. An unenriched catalog answers with nothing, and the brief then says an exclusion cannot be named here instead of printing an empty list.
+- **The determined half is scored on the document the engine would write**: `log.to_performed` on both sides, then the same comparison. Provenance travels through it, so `reported` on a prescribed number still fails — what stops failing is emitting a block the report did not mention.
+  - **Resolved blocks are matched by their exercises, not by position.** `to_performed` puts the blocks the report named first, in the order the user named them, and the untouched prescribed ones after; so the resolved order is a fact about the sentence and not about the workout, and comparing position 1 against position 1 would fail a correct agent for mentioning the third block first. The exception is a document with no `workout_id` — U8 — where nothing is filled in and the order is the only thing *"poi"* says: there, the blocks are still a sequence.
+- **Everything that currently claims the scoring is on the emitted form** changes with it: `docs/logging/eval-set.md` § How to read a row, `log_evaluation.py`'s module docstring, and `cli.eval_command`'s *"scored by equality on the document the agent emitted"*. **The thirty rows do not change.** The set's standard is the outcome the row declares, and the shape of the emission was never it — `verify` already recomputes each row's volume off the resolved document.
+
+## What this ticket does not do
+
+- **It does not touch `log.py`'s shape.** Making `rounds` and `rest` nullable, with null meaning *as prescribed*, would make the brief's own rule — *a value nobody gave is left out, not filled in* — true instead of impossible. It also rewrites every expected document in a set that was written blind, which is a bigger decision than this ticket and belongs in an ADR if it is ever taken.
+- **It does not promise a rate.** 05 runs the pass and writes the number down. What this ticket owes is a contract an agent can satisfy, each half of it checkable without a provider.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- [ ] Two documents that differ only in labelling a copied round count `reported` or `assumed` go through the scorer offline, and only the `assumed` one holds.
+- [ ] A scripted run whose first emission `log.validate` refuses reaches a document on its second, and its record shows two attempts.
+- [ ] A scripted run that spends both attempts closes once, with `REFUSED`, and writes one run document.
+- [ ] The pattern and joint values in the brief are read from the fixture catalog: a test that adds a pattern to the fixture finds it in the system message, and nothing under `src/trainer/coach` holds a list of patterns or joints.
+- [ ] Every expected document in the set is accepted by `log.validate` against the suite's own fixture — a row the contract refuses is a row no agent can hold.
+- [ ] A document that writes out all three prescribed blocks, correctly labelled, holds the row that expects one block; one that writes a round count the prescription does not say still fails it.
+- [ ] `trainer eval --suite log --only U13 --only U14 --only U16 --only U12 --repetitions 1` on `LLM_BACKEND=groq`, with its output pasted here: the two rows the vocabulary and the loop are for, and two that were failing on the form. Four rows on a fast provider, so this is not closed blind — and it is not the pass.
+- [ ] The full test suite and mypy are green.
+
+## Comments
+
+**Where the numbers come from**: the first pass against the graph, 2026-09-22, three repetitions of thirty rows on `gpt-oss:20b`, and the rescore of its recorded documents. The 40 runs missing from 90 − 50 are the must-ask half; four determined runs did not reach a document at all.
+
+**The must-ask half is not clean either until clause 1 lands.** `_filled` reads a claim off `reported` and `asked`, and charges `CONFIDENT` double for one. A model forced to label a copied prescribed value `reported` is charged for false confidence about a number the prescription supplied — so the twelve rows' second column is measuring the contract too.
+
+**This is a sixth item in a session the slicing rule caps at five.** Item 4 is withdrawn (ADR-0008), so five are live. If the number matters more than the slice, fold it into 05 and keep the two halves as its own headings — but then 05 closes on code *and* a measurement, and it is the measurement that gets cut when the context runs out.
